cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "FusionFlux: extrapolation, long-range saturation and physics constraints in confinement scaling"
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Liam
    family-names: Salcedo
    alias: lsalcedo100
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0001-5039-8147"
repository-code: "https://github.com/lsalcedo100/FusionFlux"
abstract: >-
  Confinement scaling laws are fitted to existing tokamaks and used to
  size ones not yet built, so what matters is accuracy on an unseen
  device. On the 6228-row file the OSF deposit distributes as the standard
  analysis set of the ITPA Global H-mode Confinement Database (DB5.2.3; 16
  devices), cross-validation that holds out discharges ranks a random
  forest 29% more accurate than a refitted power law. Holding out whole
  devices reverses the ranking: the forest is worse than the power law on
  all 11 devices with enough data to score. The reversal survives, though
  not on every device, when stored energy replaces confinement time as the
  target and, in a diagnostic that is not blind to the target, when the
  features are replaced by dimensionless groups that carry less device
  identity. It reproduces on all 7 eligible devices of the stellarator-
  heliotron confinement database, in an analysis fixed before those data
  were downloaded. At a size cut matching ITER's 1.82-fold step in major
  radius, 90% split-conformal intervals cover 3% and 0.3% of held-out rows
  for the two tree ensembles, with widths that change by under 1.5%, so
  they give no sign of failing. Across that cut, Connor-Taylor constraints
  on the exponents and a Gaussian process with a linear kernel term both
  reduce the error. An ablation that varies only the long-range mean is
  consistent with saturation, rather than flexibility alone, as the main
  failure mechanism in the models tested. Four reporting checks follow,
  three of which need no extra fitting.
  Includes a rank-deficiency audit of the design matrix, a from-scratch
  least-squares implementation (Cholesky, QR, SVD), a refit of the
  IPB98(y,2) exponents decomposed along the singular directions of the data,
  and a domain-agnostic module packaging the audit for other scaling-law
  datasets.
keywords:
  - fusion
  - tokamak
  - plasma physics
  - energy confinement time
  - scaling law
  - machine learning
  - extrapolation
  - out-of-distribution generalization
  - Gaussian processes
  - allometry
  - model selection
